Q27. What is the difference between abstract class and interface?
Abstract :
 - Methods → Can have both abstract methods (no body) and concrete methods (with body).
 - Fields/Variables → Can have instance variables and static variables.
 - Multiple Inheritance → ❌ Not possible (a class can extend only one abstract class).
 - Object Creation → ❌ Cannot create object directly, but subclass objects can be created.
 - Constructors → ✅ Yes, abstract classes can have constructors (used to initialize fields when subclass object is created).
  eg - 
    abstract class Animal {
    String name;
   // Abstract class constructor
    Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
        System.out.println("Animal constructor called: " + name);
    }

    class Dog extends Animal {
    Dog(String name) {
        // Calls Animal(String name) constructor
        super(name);
        System.out.println("Dog constructor called");
    }

Interface :
 - An interface in Java is like a contract that only contains method declarations (without implementation) and constants, + Default/Static/Private (Java 8+)
 - Fields/Variables → Only public static final (constants). No instance variables.
 - Object Creation → ❌ Cannot create objects. Can only be implemented by a class.
 - Multiple Inheritance → ✅ Possible (a class can implement multiple interfaces).
 - Constructors → ❌ Interfaces cannot have constructors.


