Q47. What is upcasting and downcasting?
Types of Type Casting:
 - Primitive Type Casting (between primitive data types like int, double, char).
 - Reference Type Casting (Class Casting) (between objects/classes).
    - Primitive Type Casting
      -----------------------
      Primitive type casting is of two types:
       a. Widening (Implicit Casting) :- Smaller → Larger data type,Done automatically by JVM (no data loss).
          eg- int x = 10;
              double y = x; 
       b. Narrowing (Explicit Casting) :- Larger → Smaller data type,Done manually by programmer using (type) casting.May cause data loss.
           eg- double a = 10.55;
               int b = (int) a;  
    - Reference Type Casting
    -------------------------
    👉 Casting between objects in the inheritance
    Class Casting is of two types:
        a. Upcasting (Implicit) :- Subclass object → Superclass reference,Always safe and automatic.
           eg- class Animal { 
                  void sound() { 
                 System.out.println("Animal sound"); 
               } }
               class Dog extends Animal {
               void sound() { System.out.println("Dog barks"); 
               } }
               Animal a = new Dog(); // upcasting (Dog → Animal)
                      a.sound(); // works
       b. Downcasting (Explicit) :- Superclass reference → Subclass reference,Must be done manually using (Subclass).Not always safe → may throw ClassCastException at runtime.
          eg-
              Animal a = new Dog(); // upcasting
              Dog d = (Dog) a;      // downcasting (explicit)
              d.bark();  // works
              Animal a2 = new Animal();
              Dog d2 = (Dog) a2;  // ❌ Runtime error: ClassCastException

