Q57. Difference Between PASS-BY-VALUE and PASS-BY-REFERENCE?
PASS-BY-VALUE :
  - A copy of the actual value is passed to the method.
  - Changes made inside the method do not affect the original value.
  - Think: Photocopy of a document → if you write on the copy, the original is safe.
  - Example: 
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int x = 5;
        changeValue(x);
        System.out.println(x);  // Output: 5 (not changed)
    }
    static void changeValue(int num) {
        num = num + 10;   // change applied to the copy
    }}
PASS-BY-REFERENCE :
   - The reference (address) of the variable is passed.
   - Changes made inside the method affect the original object.
   - Think: Giving someone your notebook to write in → whatever they write in it, is still in your notebook.
   - Example :
public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {1, 2, 3};
        changeArray(nums);
        System.out.println(nums[0]);  // Output: 99 (changed)
    } 
  static void changeArray(int[] arr) {
        arr[0] = 99;  // change applied to original array
    }}
Note:- Java is strictly Pass by Value :
                    a. For primitives (int, double, char, etc.) → value itself is copied.
                    b. For objects/arrays → the reference (address) value is copied, so both point to the same object. That’s why it looks like pass by reference, but technically it is still pass by value (value of the reference).


Q58. Explain Difference between IIB and SIB block ?
| Feature            | **IIB (Instance Initializer Block)**                                                | **SIB (Static Initializer Block)**                                                |
| ------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Definition**     | A block of code that runs **every time an object is created**.                      | A block of code that runs **only once when the class is loaded**.                 |
| **Keyword**        | No keyword, just `{ }` inside the class.                                            | No keyword, just `static { }`.                                                    |
| **Execution Time** | Executes **before the constructor**, each time an object is instantiated.           | Executes **once** when the class is loaded into memory.                           |
| **Access**         | Can access **instance variables** and **static variables**.                         | Can access **static variables only**; cannot access instance variables directly.  |
| **Use Case**       | Initialize instance variables or perform common operations for all objects.         | Initialize static variables or perform tasks that should run once for the class.  |


Q59. How will you prevent a class not to be cloned?
- to prevent a class from being cloned, you can override the clone() method from the Object class and throw a CloneNotSupportedException.
- Key Points:
       a. Every class in Java inherits the clone() method from Object.
       b. By default, Object.clone() allows shallow copy.
       c. To prevent cloning:
                        (i) Override clone().
                        (ii) Throw CloneNotSupportedException.
       d. Optionally, do not implement Cloneable at all. If a class does not implement Cloneable and someone calls clone(), it automatically throws CloneNotSupportedException.














